  "name": "Question 3",
  "problem": "A store manager is choosing $3$ employees to do the morning, afternoon, and night shifts. If the store has $9$ employees and each can work a single shift, how many ways of choosing the $3$ employees does the manager have?",
  "worked_solution": "The number of permutations of $n$ objects taken $r$ at a time is given by the formula\n\n$$\n{}_{n}{P}_{r}=\\frac{n!}{(n-r)!}.\n$$\n\nThere are $9$ employees to choose from, so $n=9.$ We want to select $3$ of them for the three different shifts, so we have $r=3.$ \n\nTherefore, the number of different choices is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{}_{9}{P}_{3} & =\\frac{9!}{(9-3)!} \\\\  & =\\frac{9!}{6!} \\\\  & =\\frac{9\\cdot 8\\cdot 7\\cdot \\cancel{6!}}{\\cancel{6!}} \\\\  & =9\\cdot 8\\cdot 7 \\\\  & =504.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
